The user wants a functioning, scientifically defensible, competitive submission, and prefers concrete progress with concise communication. On the latest turn the user explicitly asked to add this updated handoff to GitHub too. **This file is now part of the repository and must be kept updated and pushed with future changes.** Use Git log/status to establish the current code revision rather than relying on an old chat claim.

## Latest work: flight video outcomes (14 Nov evening, teammate's session)

Agreed direction: read NASA's BASS-II flight videos and turn them into the burn outcomes the experimental table lacks. This is the first of two agreed features; the second is the **results map** (outcomes plotted by oxygen and airflow, with ISS and lunar conditions marked), not started yet.

What exists now:
- `scripts/video_outcomes.py` — one script, five commands: `index` (maps NASA's 296 BASS-II MP4s to table tests via the public PSI API → `data/video-index.json`), `fetch` (downloads clips into git-ignored `work/videos/`), `measure` (per-frame trace per test → `data/video-traces/<test>.csv` + `<test>.json` provenance), `outcomes` (rebuilds `data/video-outcomes.csv` and `video-data.js` from saved traces, no video needed), `glyphs` (one-off: built `data/overlay-glyphs.json`).
- Per frame (4 fps): flame pixels = brightest channel exceeds the clip's per-pixel minimum by 40/255, below the on-screen overlay. Frames without the BASS overlay (camera playback spliced onto some clips) are excluded, and so are frames with the work lights on (a large grey area; flames and the igniter glow are strongly coloured).
- The overlay is read on every frame by template matching (65 labelled character shapes from B1; grid and ~1 % width differences located per clip): FUEL, AIR, FAN, RAD (radiometer) and the BASS clock. Each field accepts only the characters it can contain, a match must beat every other character by 8 px, and specks smaller than the smallest glyph count as blank. Unreadable characters make the field blank, never a guess.
- Per test: visible flame start/end/duration, flicker-tolerant flame periods, extinction-time sensitivity at thresholds 30/40/50, left/right image-edge drift (px/s, Theil-Sen), fan display at flame start/end, BASS clock at start/end, and an **independent radiometer check** (does radiation fall within 6 s of the camera losing the flame?).
- Status values: `flame went out in view, confirmed by radiometer`, `visible flame lost; radiometer says still burning` (review), `flame went out in view`, `still burning at clip end`, `no sustained flame detected`, `no readable video`.
- UI: NASA Evidence table has a "Flight video" column; the record detail panel shows a Flight video block (status, times, radiometer, fan, edge drift, chart of camera flame size vs radiometer). Files: `video-data.js` (generated), `video-outcomes.js`, two hooks in `experiment-data.js`, styles appended to `experiment-data.css`.
- Check: `python checks/video_outcomes_check.py` (synthetic flame periods, radiometer logic, template self-consistency, dataset/bundle integrity, B1 reference values).

Current coverage (14 Nov): 72 tests downloaded (Bhattacharjee, Ferkul, T'ien, Olson; Miller and Fernandez-Pello not yet, ~4.7 GB). 65 main clips measured: 25 flame went out with the radiometer agreeing, 30 camera lost a dim flame while the radiometer shows burning (review), 5 went out without radiometer confirmation (4 flagged), 5 still burning at clip end. 3 NASA main clips unreadable, 3 tests have no main clip, 1 has two.

Findings so far (verified by eye on contact sheets):
- NASA's main clips for B14, B18 and B20 are truncated (B14 stops at exactly 3 MiB) and do not decode; recorded in `note`. Worth reporting to PSI. Second-view clips are never measured: those checked (B14, O17) are handheld footage of the lab monitors.
- Several clips start or end with the work lights on (e.g. B3–B5, T5); those grey frames are excluded, so flame start times are after the lights go off.
- T13's overlay is framed differently (~130 px left, ~1.3× wider) and is not found; its outcome is flagged for review with no radiometer check.
- Where the flame stays bright, camera and radiometer agree: radiation falls 1–5 s after the camera loses the flame (sensor cooling).
- Near-limit flames turn dim and blue. The camera loses them while the radiometer (and NASA's still photos spliced at the end of some clips, e.g. B22) shows burning continues. Those tests are flagged `review`, not reported as extinguished. Lowering the threshold does not fix this (dim flame ≈ 2× noise in T1); upgrade path noted in the script.
- In several Bhattacharjee tests the flame went out while the fan display was being reduced (e.g. B1 62 → 46), so the fan reading at extinction is a flow-limit observation, not just a setting.

How to continue (Vaidehi):
1. `python scripts/video_outcomes.py outcomes` rebuilds everything from the committed traces; `python checks/video_outcomes_check.py` must pass.
2. To add tests: `python scripts/video_outcomes.py fetch --pi Olson` (or `--all`; ~6.8 GB total, NASA's host is slow), then `measure`. Several `measure` runs on different tests can run in parallel.
3. Tests with two clips of the same view (B15 M10 O5a O13a O16 P5 P12 P20 T3 T7) are skipped; choose the clip by hand if needed. 11 tests have no main clip (B17 F2 M3 O10 O17 O20 O21 O22 P8 P10 T20).
4. Next feature: the results map, built from `data/video-outcomes.csv` joined to the table's initial O₂ and flow fields. Keep `review` tests visually distinct; do not convert pixels to mm without a scale.

## Challenge expectations and submission

Official brief: https://www.spaceappschallenge.org/2026/challenges/flame-in-freefall/
Submission guide: https://www.spaceappschallenge.org/resources/project-submission-guide/

The requested product is an interactive SI-powered web dashboard that summarizes, ranks, and interprets NASA combustion investigations for fire-safety research supporting human space exploration. The brief suggests identifying relevant investigations for spacecraft, stations, lunar/Martian habitats and rovers; extracting data that can support predictive modeling; and highlighting work on prevention, detection, suppression, and equipment. These are opportunities, not a requirement to invent a predictive model.

The brief emphasizes higher oxygen/reduced pressure for lunar habitats (30% or greater O2, 10.2 psi or less) and the problem of transferring evidence across environments. BASS/BASS-II and Saffire concern solid materials; SAME/SAME-R concern smoke; FLEX concerns suppression; ACME includes gaseous-flame studies. CFI-G gas-flame context is not interchangeable with solid-material evidence. The brief's statement about future partial-gravity PSI data must be rechecked before making a current availability claim.

The user supplied the November 13, 2026 submission guide as an attachment, which was read directly. Required project fields include summary, demo link, final-project link, detailed description, use of SI, NASA data, and other references. The demo must be a video of 30 seconds or less or an English deck of at most seven slides including title/intro. Linked materials and the submission page must be English; non-English video speech requires English subtitles. Hosted links must be public. Judging criteria are **Impact, Creativity, Validity, Relevance, Presentation**. Team name, members, Local Event, and exact applicable deadline are not yet confirmed in this project; obtain them from the team's page/Local Lead. Do not infer them from a Windows username or timezone.

## Current user experience

Three workspaces replace the original long all-at-once page:

1. **Flame Lab:** start with the real NASA B3 case, try a labeled synthetic demo, or import local images; inspect an image-boundary measurement, sensitivity traces, frame overlays and CSV.
2. **Mission Review:** configure a hypothetical scenario, inspect an evidence analogue and missing conditions, export a review note or proposed test matrix; compare a target habitat's environment against the recorded evidence envelope.
3. **NASA Evidence:** filter 129 original NASA test records, inspect all source fields and quality states, export data with provenance; then ask questions of eight curated NASA findings.

Workspace navigation supports selected state, keyboard arrows/Home/End, hash links, browser history, reduced motion, visible focus, and mobile layouts. Core processing stays on the device. External citations need internet access. There is still **no verified publicly hosted interactive demo URL**; a public source repository is not the hosted demo.

## NASA source data and provenance

Investigation: PSI-25, BASS-II. DOI: https://doi.org/10.60555/4qc4-de67
Landing page: https://psi.nasa.gov/physci/repo/data/investigations/PSI-25
NASA metadata lists CC0-1.0. See `data/PROVENANCE.md`.

### Original experimental table

Original filename: `PSI-25_Experimental table_BASS-II.csv`, file version 1.
Unchanged local copy: `data/PSI-25_Experimental-table_BASS-II.csv`.
Retrieved 2026-11-14 from the public repository download endpoint.
Size: 16,590 bytes.
SHA-256: `71e792bf6b5fb5ea620c5dd70fbbac05515992e12aefcf5dff47312caa48c042`.

There are 129 unique investigator/test records, six named investigators and 17 original columns. Source cells remain strings without correction, imputation, averaging, or silent unit conversion. Record ordinals count data records, not physical CSV lines, because the file contains blank lines.

Recorded initial calibrated O2 spans **13.6–22.2%**, refining the report's approximate 14–21% overview. Scenario and transfer tools now use the actual envelope in `FIRELINE_DATA.meta.oxygenRange`. It spans heterogeneous configurations and does not establish joint-condition or specific damage-event coverage.

Final CO quality: **120 exact numeric, three censored, one other status, five missing/none**. NASA's `BASS-II_ReadMe.docx` explicitly defines `OR` as over range (>1000 ppm), so the two OR entries are treated as censored along with one >500 entry. The raw word `over` remains an uninterpreted status. None is converted to zero. Negative recorded initial CO remains intact and is flagged. Final CO is an endpoint, not necessarily a peak or emission rate. Sample reuse and heterogeneous conditions prevent assumptions of independent replicates or causation.

Filters cover investigator, material/test/sample text, and oxygen range. Exact numeric final CO is plotted against initial O2. Censored/status/missing records remain in the table. Selecting a row or plot point exposes all 17 fields. CSV exports add record ordinal, DOI, source filename/version, retrieval date and source hash. The unchanged NASA CSV is downloadable separately.

### Real B3 image case

Original file: `PSI-25_Raw Data_GMT2014_050_BASS_S148M_TPB3_ProofSheet.pdf`.
Archived unchanged as `data/BASS-II_B3_ProofSheet.pdf`.
SHA-256: `7c8521870c90786ea7e201870cfeaa37c877192d4fc312d83c530ce41d2fe0ed`.
Related NASA readme: `data/BASS-II_ReadMe.docx`.

The contact sheet identifies B3, sample 148M, 2014-02-19. The experimental table calls the sample 148; both original labels are retained. Each thumbnail is reconstructed from six embedded strips, in original order, without resizing. Dimensions are 314×209. The PDF has 43 contact-sheet images; the preset selects 11 post-ignition frames from photo clocks 12:20:37 through 12:20:58. Offsets are **0, 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 21 seconds**. The unequal gaps are used directly. No fixed-cadence assumption or silent photo/video-clock alignment is applied.

`nasa-case.js` embeds PNG data URLs for offline use. `data/B3-case-manifest.json` retains frame IDs, clocks, dimensions, source and per-image hashes. `scripts/build-nasa-case.py` reproduces the bundle from the archived PDF; its authoring dependencies are pypdf and Pillow.

Exposure varies. These are contact-sheet reproductions, not full-resolution originals. No spatial scale or independent boundary annotations have been established for this preset. The built-in preset locks timing, scale and source fields to prevent accidental misrepresentation; results stay in pixels. An optical boundary and its linearity are not automatically a physical material flame front or validated spread rate.

### Report findings

The eight curated notes are primarily from NASA/TM-20210011385:
https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20210011385

The independent image method is inspired by §2.2.4, printed p. 22: band-averaged intensity and threshold tracking. Fireline does not reproduce NASA's original MATLAB code, so the report's accuracy/uncertainty claims do not transfer to it. The approximately 100 W prolonged opposed-slab damage example and approximately 110 W brief concurrent-rod example show differing duration/geometry/heat-path context. They establish no universal wattage threshold.

The 49 MB report remains only in workspace `work/`; it is not unnecessarily rehosted in Git. No multi-GB original image archive has been downloaded.

## How the software works

`bass-data.js` is generated from the unchanged CSV by `scripts/import-bass.py`. `experiment-data.js` classifies each CO value strictly, renders source records and plots exact numeric values, handles filtering/record selection, and exports raw cells with provenance. It does not infer a risk score.

`flame-lab.js` provides the image pipeline. Imported images require equal spacing supplied by the user; the NASA preset uses its source timestamps. Images are limited to 2–60 PNG/JPEG/WEBP files, 10 MB each, 4096 pixels per side and 12 megapixels, with matching dimensions. The tool averages luminance (0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B) across a selected horizontal band and finds the outermost left/right threshold crossing with subpixel interpolation. A least-squares line fits image-boundary position versus time. Missing or border-clipped detections are excluded from the velocity fit. Without calibration the result is signed px/s; user-calibrated imports can show mm/s.

Threshold ±10 extractions expose processing sensitivity. They are not confidence intervals. R² describes linearity, not measurement accuracy. Overlays and frame inspection make processing visible. CSV retains image ID, time, source, synthetic flag, band/threshold/calibration settings, timing mode, photo clock, and source hash. A link from the NASA case filters the original table to B3. The separate synthetic demo has no NASA observations and remains explicitly labeled.

`evidence.js` supplies curated notes. `app.js` expands question terms using a small synonym map, scores topic tags, source tier and mission-lens relevance, and selects a bounded topic-based synthesis. **Current SI is deterministic retrieval and rules, not a foundation model or validated predictive intelligence.** The UI must never describe it as a trained neural model.

Scenario analysis is a heuristic structural comparison using geometry, oxygen envelope, duration and heat path. It cannot identify all conditions of the damage event or predict an outcome. Qualitative flow labels are not calibrated test velocities. The next-test recommendation is a conditional rule, not quantified information gain. The matrix's oxygen levels 14 and 21 are proposed exploratory design choices. The transfer map separately flags oxygen, gravity, missing pressure context, and material/flame-family differences. Exported briefs preserve source context.

