## Verified behavior and its limits

Calculation checks pass interpolated boundaries, missing/clipped detections, band averaging, insufficient/constant fits, and exact known synthetic motion. Dataset checks pass the CSV hash, 129 unique records, 17 fields, six investigators, oxygen range and quality handling. NASA-case checks verify source-PDF hash, all 11 image hashes/dimensions, clocks/manifest and unequal-time regression.

Automated headless Chrome QA (separate from the user's browser) passed **13 checks**: real case loading; frame/time inspection; timing/provenance CSV; B3 record linkage; 129 rows and 120 exact numeric plotted points; empty state; invalid filter bounds; raw/censored export; byte-identical source download; lunar/ISS/Mars preset updates; question prompt updates; all three workspaces at 390 px; no uncaught page errors. See `data/browser-checks.json`. Screenshots were visually inspected at desktop and mobile sizes.

The B3 preset at its default settings yields about **4.804 px/s, 11 usable frames, R²≈0.998**. This is a reproducible pipeline result on real NASA thumbnails, not error against a known physical truth. Independent boundary-annotation and spatial-calibration accuracy remain unverified. Manual imports and every failure branch have not all had a browser walkthrough.

Browser history: native Computer Use previously stopped because Chrome's current URL was uncertain. No attempt was made to bypass that restriction. Later QA uses a separately launched headless profile on this local app, and does not inspect/control the user's logged-in session.

Run from repo root:
```text
node checks/flame-core.cjs
node checks/bass-data.cjs
node checks/nasa-case.cjs
```
Optional: install playwright-core, set FIRELINE_CHROME if Chrome is elsewhere, run `node checks/browser.cjs`. A module-path argument and output-directory argument support workspace-isolated dependencies. The browser check writes screenshots/downloads/report to .qa-output by default.

## Remaining work in priority order

1. **Public demo and submission:** deploy a static build, verify anonymous access, add the URL, team/member details and demo URL. Record a coherent <=30-second walkthrough: real B3 images → visible extraction/source clocks → linked NASA record and quality handling → mission evidence gap.
2. **Scientific validation:** obtain full-resolution, time-traceable images with spatial calibration and independently annotated boundaries. Compare error across held-out frames, document annotation uncertainty, camera/exposure effects and failures. Do not relabel the current fit as physical accuracy.
3. **Robustness:** test manual image imports, invalid file dimensions/types, numeric-field edits, all export branches and browser compatibility. Add ROI cropping/masking and camera-motion checks before broader claims.
4. **Evidence coverage:** incorporate a complementary verified PSI investigation, preserving source fields, units and configuration. Keep gas-flame and solid-material experiments distinct.
5. **Grounded synthesis:** improve inspectable source retrieval across actual investigations. Add a language model only with citation constraints and evaluation; preserve offline behavior.
6. **Predictive research only if justified later:** define a target, validated comparable dataset, baseline, held-out evaluation and uncertainty. The current dashboard does not qualify a material, set alarms, or prescribe crew action.

The user wants a prize-worthy result. The credible contribution is the reproducible source-to-measurement-to-review workflow and transparent data quality. There is no basis for promising an award or claiming validated SI/fire prediction.

